  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Thursday)\nIssued at 213 PM CDT FRI SEP 18 2026\n\nA large upper ridge is currently stretched from SRN Illinois to \nMexico and be centered over North Texas. At the same time a bit \nof energy has broken off over the Western Atlantic from the main \nnorthern upper flow and is becoming a closed low over Florida. \nThe low should slowly nudge westward over the short term and then \nhelp to push and degrade the ridge. This will transition our \nweather pattern and finally yield a return to some, at least low, \nrain chances. As the upper low moves closer to us, low rain \nchances have returned for this afternoon assisted by daytime \nheating. Initially, this weekend, overall moisture will be \nlimited, so QPF amounts are low. Some storms may be strong to\nsevere on Saturday afternoon and evening with the main threat\nbeing gusty winds. For Sunday into Monday, guidance brings the \nupper low further westward over the Northern Gulf Coast. This will\nbegin to allow for continued moisture to filter into the state. \nLow to medium chances for isolated to scattered convection will \noccur but with still only meager rainfall amounts for a lucky few.\nOn Tuesday, guidance weakens/deteriorates the low. Diurnal pop \nchances continue through mid week and the end of our forecast \nahead of an approaching surface front/boundary. Unfortunately, \noverall moisture continues to be limited ahead of this system with\nonly minimal rainfall amounts forecasted. So, over the next week,\nno drought busting rainfall is expected. However, if the front\ndoes make it through on Wednesday into Thursday, then temperatures\nwill be able to go down to \"normal\" for this time of year. \n\nAs far as temperatures are concerned over the next week, the\nwarmest day will be tomorrow, on Saturday. Temperatures will be \nhot with heat indices as high as 100-106 at times. Guidance\ntrended a tad warmer for Saturday. Decided to go with a heat \nadvisory for most of Central Alabama (all but far east/southeast) \nfor Saturday afternoon, noon through 8pm. Concern is for all of \nthe outdoor activities and that it is much warmer than normal for \nthis time of year (outlier event). While we do have scattered \nstorm chances in the forecast, outside of storms will be toasty. \nPlan accordingly!\n\n08",
